Chapter: CloudRender
  cites Clouds chapter CloudModel
  cites Water chapter OceanModel
  cites Foreword chapter Console

 Draws the cloud model to the GOP framebuffer: one ray per traced sample,
 composited over a sky gradient.

 It traces at HALF resolution and writes 2x2 blocks. That is not laziness,
 it is the cost class. The ocean renderer evaluates its model once per grid
 vertex and fills the triangles between them, so its shading cost is set by
 the grid and not by the screen. A raymarch has no such structure: every
 pixel walks the volume itself, and each step of that walk evaluates a
 fractal sum which is eight lattice hashes per octave. At full resolution
 with the light march included that is a few billion hashes for one frame.
 Halving each axis is a factor of four for a picture whose content is
 low-frequency anyway, which is the right trade and is why real engines
 trace clouds at quarter resolution and upscale.

 What keeps it tractable at all is that most rays do almost nothing. A step
 below the density floor never runs the light march, and `c-density` returns
 before evaluating the detail sum whenever the base shape has already lost.
 The expensive path is only paid inside cloud.

Section: Framebuffer

 The standing GOP surface codex-vm renders at 0xBF000000: one 32-bit XRGB
 pixel per cell, row-major, no pitch padding. A write outside the bounds is
 dropped rather than wrapped. The resolution here must match the
 `-gop-width` and `-gop-height` the machine is booted with; the command in
 the README passes both.

  cr-fb-base : Integer
  cr-fb-base = #BF000000

  cr-width : Integer
  cr-width = 640

  cr-height : Integer
  cr-height = 400

 The traced grid. Each traced sample fills a 2x2 block.

  cr-tw : Integer
  cr-tw = 320

  cr-th : Integer
  cr-th = 200

  cr-half-w : Real
  cr-half-w = 160.0

  cr-half-h : Real
  cr-half-h = 100.0

  cr-focal : Real
  cr-focal = 240.0

  cr-pack : Real, Real, Real -> Integer
  cr-pack (r) (g) (b) =
    let ri = real-to-int (w-sat r * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let gi = real-to-int (w-sat g * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in let bi = real-to-int (w-sat b * 255.0 + 0.5)
    in ri * 65536 + gi * 256 + bi

  cr-put : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-put (x) (y) (color) =
    if x < 0 then 0
    else if y < 0 then 0
    else if x >= cr-width then 0
    else if y >= cr-height then 0
    else poke-32 cr-fb-base ((y * cr-width + x) * 4) color

 The buffer is cleared to a flat colour before drawing. The GOP surface
 comes up as whatever was in RAM, which is zeros, and zeros are black; a
 capture that fires before the last row is drawn then shows a black band
 that is indistinguishable from a rendered night sky. Clearing to a colour
 that appears nowhere else in the image makes an unfinished frame obvious
 instead of plausible. That is not hypothetical -- the first capture of this
 scene was a partial draw and read as a horizon.

  cr-clear-color : Integer
  cr-clear-color = #FF00FF

  cr-clear-row : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-clear-row (x) (y) =
    if x >= cr-width then 0
    else let d = cr-put x y cr-clear-color
    in cr-clear-row (x + 1) y

  cr-clear : Integer -> Integer
  cr-clear (y) =
    if y >= cr-height then 0
    else let d = cr-clear-row 0 y
    in cr-clear (y + 1)

  cr-put-block : Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-put-block (tx) (ty) (color) =
    let x = tx * 2
    in let y = ty * 2
    in let a = cr-put x y color
    in let b = cr-put (x + 1) y color
    in let c = cr-put x (y + 1) color
    in cr-put (x + 1) (y + 1) color

Section: Camera

 A right-handed basis built from a forward direction. The camera looks
 along +z and a little above the horizon, turned toward the sun so the
 forward-scattering rim the phase function produces is actually in frame.
 A cloud shader pointed away from its own sun looks like nothing at all.

  Cam = record { cf : V3, cr : V3, cu : V3 }

  cr-cross : V3, V3 -> V3
  cr-cross (a) (b) =
    V3 {
      x = a.y * b.z - a.z * b.y,
      y = a.z * b.x - a.x * b.z,
      z = a.x * b.y - a.y * b.x
    }

 The pitch matters more than anything else in this file. A camera looking
 along the slab rather than up into it sends every ray through kilometres of
 cloud, and a 2500 m deck seen edge-on saturates to opaque everywhere: the
 first framing here used a pitch of 0.15 and produced a flat grey wall with
 no shape in it at all. Looking up puts the ray across the slab instead of
 along it, which is where the height gradient and the erosion become
 visible.

  cr-eye : V3
  cr-eye = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 300.0, z = 0.0 }

  cr-world-up : V3
  cr-world-up = V3 { x = 0.0, y = 1.0, z = 0.0 }

  cr-forward : V3
  cr-forward = V3 { x = 0.30, y = 0.50, z = 0.81 }

  cr-camera : Cam
  cr-camera =
    let f = v-norm cr-forward
    in let r = v-norm (cr-cross cr-world-up f)
    in Cam { cf = f, cr = r, cu = cr-cross f r }

  cr-ray : Cam, Integer, Integer -> V3
  cr-ray (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    let sx = (real-from-int tx - cr-half-w + 0.5) / cr-focal
    in let sy = (cr-half-h - real-from-int ty - 0.5) / cr-focal
    in let a = v-add (cam.cf) (v-scale (cam.cr) sx)
    in v-norm (v-add a (v-scale (cam.cu) sy))

Section: Sky

 The background the clouds are composited over. Zenith to horizon by
 height, plus the sun's disc and its halo.

 The disc and the halo are the same term at two exponents. `w-powi` is a
 chain of multiplies, so the exponents are kept small deliberately: 64 is a
 tight enough disc at this focal length and costs 64 multiplies, where the
 200 a sharper edge would want costs 200 on every pixel of the sky.

  cr-ground : V3
  cr-ground = V3 { x = 0.21, y = 0.23, z = 0.19 }

  cr-sun-glow : Real
  cr-sun-glow = 0.22

  cr-sky : V3 -> V3
  cr-sky (dir) =
    if dir.y < 0.0 then cr-ground
    else let t = w-sat (dir.y * 1.6)
    in let base = v-lerp cloud-horizon-color cloud-sky-color t
    in let s = w-sat (v-dot dir (v-norm cloud-sun))
    in let disc = w-powi s 64
    in let halo = w-powi s 8 * cr-sun-glow
    in v-add base (v-scale cloud-sun-color (disc + halo))

Section: The Frame

 One traced sample: trace the clouds, composite over the sky. The march
 already carries premultiplied scattered light, so the composite is the
 straight `scattered + background * transmittance` and needs no divide.

  cr-sample : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-sample (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    let dir = cr-ray cam tx ty
    in let sky = cr-sky dir
    in let m = c-trace-at cr-eye dir (c-jitter-at tx ty)
    in let cr = m.r + sky.x * m.trans
    in let cg = m.g + sky.y * m.trans
    in let cb = m.b + sky.z * m.trans
    in cr-pack cr cg cb

  cr-row : Cam, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-row (cam) (tx) (ty) =
    if tx >= cr-tw then 0
    else let c = cr-put-block tx ty (cr-sample cam tx ty)
    in cr-row cam (tx + 1) ty

  cr-rows : Cam, Integer -> Integer
  cr-rows (cam) (ty) =
    if ty >= cr-th then 0
    else let d = cr-row cam 0 ty
    in cr-rows cam (ty + 1)

Section: Holding the Frame

 `-screenshot` captures at `-screenshot-delay` and ends the run there. Both
 ways of missing it were hit while building this, and they fail in opposite
 directions, so the window matters more than the delay does:

   delay SHORTER than the draw -- the capture lands mid-frame. Rows are
     written top to bottom, so the result is a plausible-looking image with
     the bottom missing. This is what `cr-clear-color` exists to expose.
   delay LONGER than the run -- the guest exits first and NO file is
     written at all.

 So the guest has to still be alive, with a finished frame, when the timer
 fires. The hold watches the PIT tick count in low memory and spins to hold
 the frame up, and it is deliberately long enough that the delay does not
 have to be guessed precisely. The fuel cap means a machine booted without a
 running timer still terminates instead of hanging the harness.

  cr-tick-addr : Integer
  cr-tick-addr = 28672

  cr-hold-ticks : Integer
  cr-hold-ticks = 220

  cr-hold-fuel : Integer
  cr-hold-fuel = 40000000000

  cr-hold : Integer, Integer -> Integer
  cr-hold (until) (fuel) =
    if fuel <= 0 then 0
    else if peek-32 cr-tick-addr 0 >= until then 1
    else cr-hold until (fuel - 1)

  cr-hold-frame : Integer
  cr-hold-frame = cr-hold (peek-32 cr-tick-addr 0 + cr-hold-ticks) cr-hold-fuel

Section: Driver

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "Cloud render (raymarched, Beer-Powder, Henyey-Greenstein)"
    print-line-uni ("resolution = " & show cr-width & "x" & show cr-height & ", traced at " & show cr-tw & "x" & show cr-th)
    print-line-uni ("view steps = " & show cloud-steps & ", light steps = " & show cloud-light-steps)
    print-line-uni ("coverage = " & show cloud-coverage & ", extinction = " & show cloud-extinction)
    let cam = cr-camera
    in let c0 = cr-clear 0
    in let d = cr-rows cam 0
    in act
      print-line-uni "drawn"
      print-line-uni ("held = " & show cr-hold-frame)
    end
  end
